 ## Approach
 
-Keep one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS; browsers display a revisioned view of the board. The approach for delivering committed changes is still open: five-second HTTPS polling, self-hosted WebSockets, or a hosted realtime service. All three can meet the required freshness target. Subsecond updates would be useful, but are not required.
+Keep one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS; browsers display a revisioned view of the board. Committed changes reach browsers over self-hosted WebSockets: the server owns the connections and pushes each committed revision as it lands, and a browser whose connection drops reconnects and catches up from the last revision it saw. Push delivery gives fast updates without tying the board to a realtime vendor; in exchange the project owns connection capacity and reconnect behavior. Subsecond updates are a welcome side effect rather than a requirement — the required bar is a fifteen-second freshness target.
 
-Delivery, change storage, and browser recovery must be designed together after this choice. Polling would favor cursor queries; self-hosted sockets would need connection management and replay; hosted delivery would need publication reconciliation and provider credentials. Neither operating cost nor minimum operational burden has been declared the overriding criterion.
+Because delivery is push-based and stateful, change storage, delivery, and browser recovery are designed together. The change store retains replayable revisions so a reconnecting browser can be brought current. Delivery manages socket sessions and publishes committed revisions reliably to connected browsers. Browser sync owns the subscription lifecycle: connecting, reconnecting after a drop, and catching up.
 
